3. ERDŐS-870 SUBBASIS TRANSFER
- The erdos-870 proof strategy is representation economy: build unique order-h representations and exclude lower-order representations.
- Scheme B is the n=4 tournament analogue of an order-2 subbasis: fixed C4 filler + two matching free bits represents T,+,-,S exactly once.
- Scheme A is the useful but unsafe tiling quotient: it keeps all three chord directions, but S has a one-generator representative and several higher-order representatives.
- Therefore the LRC packet theorem should ask for a quotient basis only after checking exact-order representation, not merely after checking class coverage.

4. LRC14 PACKET THEOREM CANDIDATE
packet_subbasis(P) = fixed_filler_sidecar + free_obstruction_basis + lower_order_exclusion + coordinate_resurrection_or_named_debt
proposed_rule=an LRC quotient is legal only if each terminal obstruction class has a unique squarefree packet word of the declared order, or if the collision fiber is named and repaired.
n4_transfer_to_current_frontier:
  two_bit_matching_basis -> finite four-point/unital-block local carrier
  fixed_C4_filler -> powers-of-two/carry-separated deterministic scaffold
  SchemeA_S_collision -> quotient debt like HYP-3138 odd leakage or HYP-3139 boundary leakage
  HYP-3141 edge packets -> attach tail/tip labels before quotienting an edge
  HYP-3142 U4 exit -> terminal bounded-core sidecar that must not be reached by a lower-order word

